 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>The final three looks of this Lacoste show featured white garments—jeans, a polo, T-shirts, shorts and sneakers—that appeared to have been passionately pressed into the ochre surface of the Philippe Chatrier center court at Roland-Garros. This streaked effect was layered over a wet-look wrinkling—a visual sweaty pressure—that designer Pelagia Kolotouros said backstage was achieved by sandwiching fabric over tulle and fusible, then applying hot steam so that it shrank and puckered permanently. The impression conveyed was of a roll in the clay: was Kolotouros working to sex up Lacoste? “Definitely,” she said. “I think sport is sexy.”</p><p>Another streak Kolotouros is trying to express through her work at Lacoste is rebellion. She hangs onto the story of René Lacoste’s then-shocking decision to play in short sleeves—very much against the conventions of 1920s tennis—as evidence of subversion in the brand’s DNA. Running counter to that, however, is the status of its most emblematic garment, the polo shirt, as a pillar of the conventional preppy wardrobe.</p><p>That contradiction was explored on Kolotouros’s moodboard, which included an old newspaper clipping headlined “Putting down preppydom” alongside anti-prep slogans including “Save an alligator, shoot a preppie.” “There’s so much prep going out there right now,” she said backstage. “I don’t like people to get caged into something that’s prescriptive.” While not shot, the crocs on her pique polos were inverted as a reference to the logo-flipping instinct she was following. They also came with metal filament in their collars and hems, allowing for popping so chaotic it would be frowned on at the country club. Her new bag featured a collar-like flap down one side. </p><p>Another influence was velocity. Kolotouros said she became fixated on the 217 km/h reading she saw on a serve-speed display while watching Novak Djokovic play in Athens in November 2025, and translated that number into graphics on pleated drop-waisted dresses and hoodies. The bulge of a net when struck by a serve informed the net-like structure encasing the upper of a new sneaker, the ACE-T1, which came with a tennis ball embedded in its heel. This shoe was also delivered at mid-heel height for night matches.</p><p>The palette was mostly olive, white and clay, with minor rallies of cobalt blue, tennis-ball green, orange and silver. Knitted ties were loosely knotted over polos. There were a few pieces in tennis-ball paillettes. Lacoste’s sleeve rebellion was referenced in looks that layered T-shirts featuring Irving Penn’s photograph of a folded croc polo over mid-sleeved clay shirts and wrist-length olive base layers.</p><p>Kolotouros said her game plan this season was to refashion the house’s polo so as to seem “more individualistic and not as pristine and perfect.” This approach carried over into the collection more broadly.</p></div>",
